  <!-- notes beside a row that is carrying something structural -->
  <data name="ThemeShadowIsStructure" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>There is almost no difference in brightness between this plate and this deck, so the shadow is the only thing separating a control from the space behind it. At zero the layout disappears.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeRestingGlowIsStructure" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A raster box is barely brighter than the glass it is drawn on, so the light it spills all the time is what makes it an object. At zero the controls dissolve into the deck.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeArcTrackNote" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A fader slot is cut into the plate, so its track is a dark. A knob's arc sits outside the plate on bare deck, where on a dark theme there is nothing left to be darker than, so it has to be a faint light. Leave this empty to use the slot track for both.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeLightColorNote" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Leave this empty and a control lights up in its own color. A phosphor does not: a monochrome tube glows blue-white, an amber one glows redder than the thing casting it, and a green one glows yellower.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeInkNote" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Leave this empty and the label color is measured against whatever the plate turned out to be. A tube names it instead, because its ink is its phosphor: a neutral white label on amber glass reads as a fault rather than as a picture.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeOverlayNote" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>One layer over the whole page rather than one per control, drawn in screen pixels so the lines stay the same distance apart at every zoom.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeMeterNote" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The one control where a heat ramp does real work. Pick three slots that are also a rising brightness and it still reads with no color vision at all.</value>
  </data>

